<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ten Years' War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ten Years' War of 1868–1878 was the first major Cuban war of independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-manuel-de-cespedes">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</a> freed his enslaved workers and proclaimed independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-demajagua">La Demajagua</a> on 10 October 1868, and was fought by the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in Arms mainly in the east of the island. It ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-zanjon">Pact of Zanjón</a>, without independence or the full abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ten-years-war.jpg" alt="Ten Years' War" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramon Padró i Pedret · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embarcament_dels_voluntaris_catalans_al_port_de_Barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The uprising was led by Cuban-born planters and other wealthy natives. Lax enforcement of the ban on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> had brought an estimated 90,000 enslaved Africans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1856 to 1860, although a strong abolitionist movement existed on the island, and planters in the east faced rising costs. In May 1865 Cuban criollo elites asked the Spanish Parliament for tariff reform, political representation and judicial equality for all Spaniards. Instead the colonial government raised taxes by six per cent and silenced political opposition and the press. Discontent was strongest among the planters of eastern Cuba, and in July 1867 the Revolutionary Committee of Bayamo was founded under Francisco Vicente Aguilera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-manuel-de-cespedes">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</a>, who owned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-demajagua">La Demajagua</a>, had planned the rising for 14 October 1868, but the Spaniards discovered the plan and he moved it forward. On 10 October he issued the 10th of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a> at La Demajagua, freed his slaves and asked them to join the struggle. The manifesto aimed at abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> with compensation, freedom of assembly and of the press, and a free and independent nation. By the end of the month some 12,000 volunteers had enlisted. The rebels took Bayamo, where Perucho Figueredo composed the anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-bayamesa">La Bayamesa</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> retook it on 12 January, after fighting had burned it down.</p><p>On 10 April 1869 an assembly at Guáimaro separated civil government from military command, and Céspedes became the first president of the Republic in Arms. The Spanish relied on the Voluntary Corps, which grew to more than 70,000 men and executed eight students of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> on 27 November 1871. Ignacio Agramonte was killed on 11 May 1873, and Céspedes was deposed and killed on 27 February 1874. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war">Third Carlist War</a> ended in 1876, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had more than 250,000 troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Tomás Estrada Palma</a> was captured on 19 October 1877. The rebel government dissolved on 8 February 1878, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsenio-martinez-campos">Arsenio Martínez Campos</a> persuaded most rebels to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-zanjon">Pact of Zanjón</a>, signed on 10 February 1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-maceo">Antonio Maceo</a> protested at Baraguá on 15 March, and the war ended on 28 May 1878. The pact promised reforms and freed the slaves who had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Spain abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1880 in a gradual form that required freed people to keep working for their masters.</p><p>About 190,000 people died, among them 100,000 Cubans and 90,000 Spaniards, mostly of disease. The Little War of 1879 to 1880 followed, and veterans such as the Maceo brothers, Gómez and Calixto García led the Cuban War of Independence from 1895. The war also devastated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ten Years' War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ten Years' War</b> of 1868-78, led by the Republic of Cuba in Arms, and the war of 1895 challenged Spanish rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-cuba">Captaincy General of Cuba</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 October 1868 the planter Carlos Manuel de Céspedes freed his enslaved workers and proclaimed independence at La Demajagua, starting the <b>Ten Years' War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a></li><li><blockquote>The government faced the Third Carlist War, the Cuban <b>Ten Years' War</b> and the Cantonal Rebellion, and its presidents, Figueras, Pi, Nicolás Salmerón and Emilio Castelar, followed in quick succession.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
